          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 3,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        }
      ],
      "why": "Only non-1-result/cycle family that could plausibly reach 50 MSPS: throughput = Fmax/(ceil(N/k)+3), so it needs a high Fmax with k=4..8 chained micro-rotations. It reuses one datapath so its area/FF count is low, which could be attractive for power if the long combinational path still clears 50 MSPS. Given the Fmax risk it gets the smallest share and k is restricted to the high end."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Register, not throughput, is what we can afford here: the spec only needs 50 MSPS, and both pipelined and pipelined_m deliver 1 result/cycle, so any sky130 clock above ~50 MHz satisfies the throughput constraint and the search should spend its budget on the real trade-off, power_index vs accuracy_bits. The accuracy side is pinned by W and N (max_abs_err <= 2^-12 means W >= 14 is needed before anything else, since the output LSB is 2^-(W-2)); n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding are all exposed because code computes the exact error and will find the cheapest combination that clears 2^-12. iterative is deliberately excluded: it needs ~N>=13 micro-rotations and N+3 cycles/result, so reaching 50 MSPS would demand a clock an order of magnitude beyond what these cells give, and no accuracy-feasible point exists in that branch. unrolled_k is kept only as a small high-k probe because its Fmax/(ceil(N/k)+3) throughput is borderline. Round 1 therefore weights pipelined_m highest (its m knob directly attacks min power_index), keeps pipelined as the guaranteed-feasible anchor, and leaves a small slice to test whether unrolled_k ever reaches the 50 MSPS floor. Ranges are kept moderately wide (W 13..20, N 10..20, guards -2..3 / 0..3) so the first round maps where the constraint boundaries actually fall before later rounds exploit them."
}
